00:03:47.582 --> 00:03:49.749
Kevin: Hey everyone, uh,
thanks for having me.

00:03:49.867 --> 00:03:54.477
This talk is called You Didn't Fix
Accessibility: You Automated the Problem.

00:03:55.666 --> 00:04:00.555
It's about AI in the WordPress ecosystem,
uh, specifically, not AI in general,

00:04:00.556 --> 00:04:05.129
and not the philosophical question of
whether AI is good or bad in the abstract.

00:04:05.593 --> 00:04:09.021
This is about what's actually happening
in production WordPress environments

00:04:09.022 --> 00:04:13.149
right now, where accessibility is
not a th- a theory or anything.

00:04:13.162 --> 00:04:17.628
It's operational, it's high risk,
and for a lot of your users, myself

00:04:17.629 --> 00:04:20.757
included, we'll get to that in a minute,
it's the difference between being

00:04:20.757 --> 00:04:22.892
able to a- uh, to use the site or not.

00:04:23.693 --> 00:04:24.980
I want to set the frame early.

00:04:25.335 --> 00:04:32.016
Uh, so everything in the next 30 to 35
minutes is scoped to three specific places

00:04:32.048 --> 00:04:34.676
AI shows up in a WordPress workflow.

00:04:34.965 --> 00:04:40.683
So we have auto-generated alt text,
automated scanning tools and overlays, and

00:04:40.688 --> 00:04:45.758
plugin-based automated fixes, including
what's happening inside Gutenberg.

00:04:46.222 --> 00:04:49.464
I'm not going to talk about large
language models, uh, at all in this

00:04:49.476 --> 00:04:54.182
talk today and, and I'm not going to
talk about AI ethics as a category,

00:04:54.183 --> 00:04:55.851
as important as those topics are.

00:04:56.471 --> 00:04:59.936
I'm going to talk about what happens
when these specific tools, um,

00:05:00.075 --> 00:05:03.244
touch a real site with real disabled
people who are trying to use it.

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:07.611
Okay, so some quick background so
you know from where this is coming.

00:05:08.114 --> 00:05:09.323
By the way, I'm Kevin Andrews.

00:05:09.323 --> 00:05:11.629
I… Make sure you're
all in the right place.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:15.364
I'm an, uh, International Association
of Accessibility Professionals

00:05:15.415 --> 00:05:17.778
certified web accessibility specialist.

00:05:17.828 --> 00:05:19.314
Try saying that five times fast.

00:05:20.151 --> 00:05:24.363
Uh, I'm currently at Georgetown
University, uh, and I've held

00:05:24.435 --> 00:05:27.182
the accessibility role here
for a little over seven years.

00:05:27.665 --> 00:05:32.451
So I audit large decentralized
WordPress environments, uh, for a

00:05:32.453 --> 00:05:36.951
living, and so I also use a screen
reader myself every day on the same

00:05:36.953 --> 00:05:38.569
kinds of sites that I'm auditing.

00:05:39.129 --> 00:05:42.569
So that distinction matters for how you
should weigh what I'm about to tell you.

00:05:43.243 --> 00:05:48.787
Uh, when I say an AI tool failed, I'm
not extrapolating from some demo video

00:05:48.797 --> 00:05:50.770
or vendor's marketing page or VPAT.

00:05:50.782 --> 00:05:51.550
More on that later.

00:05:51.726 --> 00:05:56.962
I was either the user on whom it
failed, or I was the one who found

00:05:56.962 --> 00:06:01.090
out it failed on someone else after
it already shipped to production.

00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:06.579
So that's a different kind of evidence
than a case study a vendor wrote about

00:06:06.585 --> 00:06:12.995
themselves, and I want you to hold
that distinction throughout this talk.

00:06:13.501 --> 00:06:14.683
So a quick example.

00:06:14.769 --> 00:06:16.234
A quick example just from this week.

00:06:16.552 --> 00:06:20.382
Uh, before we get into it- This
happened within the last week or so.

00:06:20.384 --> 00:06:24.517
The speaker prep email for this
very conference linked to a guide

00:06:24.521 --> 00:06:27.199
on how to look fabulous on a webcam.

00:06:27.712 --> 00:06:33.009
So camera at eye level, key lighting,
ward- wardrobe, colors that pop on screen.

00:06:33.615 --> 00:06:34.586
So I use a screen reader.

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.692
None of that was written for me in mind.

00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:40.634
I, I… Sorry, I don't think
anyone really meant anything by it.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.811
Uh, that's actually the whole point I
want to make with this example before

00:06:44.812 --> 00:06:46.432
we get into the AI content, though.

00:06:46.965 --> 00:06:50.834
So it's an easy trap to fall into,
uh, with the best of intentions.

00:06:51.207 --> 00:06:56.296
So a template goes out to everyone on
the speaker list because that's efficient

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:00.104
and that's the default, and nobody
checks whether everyone, air quotes,

00:07:01.041 --> 00:07:03.044
actually includes everyone on that list.

00:07:03.198 --> 00:07:08.731
So nobody sat down and asked
who was receiving, uh, a-

00:07:08.741 --> 00:07:09.704
and does that serve them?

00:07:10.206 --> 00:07:11.950
So that's really the whole talk actually.

00:07:12.952 --> 00:07:14.515
Just not the AI version of this yet.

00:07:15.099 --> 00:07:16.158
So hold onto that thought.

00:07:16.263 --> 00:07:20.472
Um, we're gonna spend some time
really dissecting the automated

00:07:20.474 --> 00:07:22.955
version of exactly that failure.

00:07:23.368 --> 00:07:29.325
And, um, uh, we just established
the pattern of real human example.

00:07:29.645 --> 00:07:34.061
Um, so before we get into it, I wanna
talk through how that same pattern

00:07:34.128 --> 00:07:40.063
shows up when the well-intentioned
default Isn't a webcam guide.

00:07:40.467 --> 00:07:44.599
It's an AI tool that promises
to solve accessibility for you.

00:07:45.373 --> 00:07:47.517
So here's the pitch,
and you've all heard it.

00:07:48.322 --> 00:07:53.199
AI is the scalable fix for accessible
de- uh, accessibility debt.

00:07:53.787 --> 00:07:55.257
Alt text writes itself now.

00:07:55.622 --> 00:07:59.402
Uh, scanning tools flag
thousands of issues in seconds.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:05.037
One-click plugins promise compliance
without touching your code bases.

00:08:06.217 --> 00:08:10.132
That's the sales pitch, and it is
a genuinely compelling one because

00:08:10.139 --> 00:08:16.374
accessibility debt on a large WordPress
site is a real expensive, unglamorous

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:18.301
problem that nobody wants to own.

00:08:19.244 --> 00:08:21.407
Speaking from ex-
firsthand experience here.

00:08:22.009 --> 00:08:26.508
This talk is about what happens when you
actually run these tools against a real

00:08:26.529 --> 00:08:32.957
messy, decentralized, Georgetown WordPress
environment instead of a demo site because

00:08:34.006 --> 00:08:36.173
those are two very different experiences.

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:41.892
A demo site has clean, it has clean
markup, it has a handful of templates,

00:08:42.384 --> 00:08:44.425
and a vendor who chose the examples.

00:08:44.972 --> 00:08:49.429
A real university on Enterprise
WordPress environment has 10 years

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:54.195
of content, 40 different content
editors with 40 different habits and

00:08:54.283 --> 00:08:56.680
plugins nobody remembers installing.

00:08:57.155 --> 00:09:01.078
That gap between the demo and the
production environment is really

00:09:01.078 --> 00:09:02.977
where this whole talk lives.

00:09:03.651 --> 00:09:07.775
So before I go any further, I want to be
really clear about what this talk is not.

00:09:08.212 --> 00:09:10.204
It is not an anti-AI talk.

00:09:10.415 --> 00:09:14.018
I'm not going to stand up here or
sit here actually and tell you AI

00:09:14.021 --> 00:09:18.440
is inherently bad because it's not
an argument, and it doesn't hold

00:09:18.441 --> 00:09:20.086
up under any s- sort of scrutiny.

00:09:20.271 --> 00:09:24.154
I'm also not here to tell you that it's
all, uh, that it's all doom, right?

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.946
There are specific, very narrow places
where these tools genuinely help, and

00:09:28.946 --> 00:09:33.860
I will point them out, um, sort of
explicitly as we go through this because

00:09:33.861 --> 00:09:38.080
pretending otherwise will make this talk
dishonest and frankly less useful to you.

00:09:38.333 --> 00:09:41.664
So what this is, is practical.

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:45.567
Where do these tools break
down specifically in WordPress?

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:50.967
Why do these, uh, why do teams keep
relying on them anyway, even after all

00:09:50.978 --> 00:09:53.068
the failure modes have been documented?

00:09:53.564 --> 00:09:56.925
And what do you actually do
about it starting Monday morning?

00:09:57.719 --> 00:10:01.330
So that's the shape that
we're going with here.

00:10:01.345 --> 00:10:02.081
So next slide.

00:10:02.631 --> 00:10:06.020
So we're going to walk through
three specific places AI shows

00:10:06.028 --> 00:10:07.419
up in a WordPress workflow.

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.982
First, automated generated
alt text in the media library.

00:10:11.341 --> 00:10:14.439
Second, automated scanning tools
and accessibility overlays.

00:10:14.721 --> 00:10:19.820
Third, plugin-based automated fixes,
including what's happening inside

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:24.505
Gutenberg with the block patterns
specifically, um, because that's a real

00:10:24.505 --> 00:10:29.072
WordPress-specific risk multiplier that
I don't think gets enough attention.

00:10:29.651 --> 00:10:34.833
So for each of the three, I'm going
to walk, uh, uh, pardon me, I'm

00:10:34.833 --> 00:10:36.113
going to walk the same structure.

00:10:36.665 --> 00:10:40.686
So what it promises, what it actually
does once you put it in front of a

00:10:40.686 --> 00:10:44.554
real user with a real screen reader
or a real keyboard-only workflow,

00:10:45.051 --> 00:10:48.990
and where the gap between those
two things puts your users at risk.

00:10:49.309 --> 00:10:54.023
Then we'll zoom out and talk about why
this pattern keeps, uh, sort of repeating

00:10:54.023 --> 00:10:58.488
across all three, and I'll show you
five guardrails you can put in place

00:10:58.507 --> 00:11:00.996
without ripping out any of the tools.

00:11:02.578 --> 00:11:02.944
All right.

00:11:03.020 --> 00:11:05.257
So let's get to it.

00:11:06.431 --> 00:11:11.751
Automated generated alt text, and
then we'll move, um, straight into it.

00:11:11.761 --> 00:11:12.520
So next slide.

00:11:15.233 --> 00:11:18.176
All right, so st-start with alt text,
uh, because it's the friendliest

00:11:18.176 --> 00:11:19.366
looking version of this problem.

00:11:19.829 --> 00:11:21.044
So the promise is simple.

00:11:21.969 --> 00:11:26.952
Upload an image, the model looks at the
fixes, it writes you a description, done.

00:11:27.646 --> 00:11:30.577
No content editor has to sit
there and think about it.

00:11:31.697 --> 00:11:33.191
No workflow bottleneck.

00:11:33.481 --> 00:11:34.355
And then this pitch…

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:37.709
And this pitch works really
because if you think about it,

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.173
the backlog problem is real.

00:11:39.676 --> 00:11:43.522
I've audited, uh, a ton of these
university libraries with tens of thou…

00:11:43.553 --> 00:11:47.688
no lie, tens of thousands of images, and
effectively no alt text on any of them.

00:11:48.367 --> 00:11:54.072
When you're the person staring down, uh,
a spreadsheet with that many rows, just

00:11:54.072 --> 00:11:59.449
let the AI handle it, in air quotes, is
a genuinely appealing sentence, and I

00:11:59.453 --> 00:12:01.105
don't fault anyone for reaching for it.

00:12:01.464 --> 00:12:02.119
Really, I don't.

00:12:02.595 --> 00:12:07.652
The problem is, uh, the problem
isn't the instinct is unreasonable.

00:12:08.086 --> 00:12:13.060
Really, the problem is what h-happens
next, which is the next, uh, few slides.

00:12:13.076 --> 00:12:14.674
So, uh, next slide

00:12:17.089 --> 00:12:17.650
Um

00:12:20.286 --> 00:12:22.723
Okay, so here's the
actual problem, though.

00:12:23.044 --> 00:12:25.872
AI describes, uh, it describes pixels.

00:12:26.125 --> 00:12:27.784
It does not know purpose.

00:12:28.015 --> 00:12:33.129
So take the same photo, uh, take the
same photo of a person at a podium.

00:12:33.547 --> 00:12:39.048
On a news page, a, uh, the, the alt text,
"A person standing at a podium," is a

00:12:39.058 --> 00:12:41.722
reasonable sort of caption, if you will.

00:12:41.875 --> 00:12:42.602
It does the job.

00:12:43.102 --> 00:12:47.117
But you put that exact same photo on
a donation page, and it's close to

00:12:47.117 --> 00:12:53.798
useless because what a screen reader user
actually needs there is, who is, uh, who

00:12:53.799 --> 00:12:58.996
is this, and why does it matter to ask,
uh, what, what you're asking of them?

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:04.465
You put it on a product page, and
you need color, fit, material,

00:13:04.807 --> 00:13:08.905
the actual sort of decision-making
detail a sighted user gets for free

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:12.747
by glancing at the image or images.

00:13:13.203 --> 00:13:18.608
So the model has no idea, uh, which
page it's on, uh, what the goal

00:13:18.608 --> 00:13:23.106
of that page is, or what decision
the alt text is supposed to sort

00:13:23.106 --> 00:13:26.913
of support because none of that is
encoded in the pixels themselves.

00:13:27.307 --> 00:13:28.447
It describes the scene.

00:13:28.653 --> 00:13:33.806
It cannot describe the job the image is
doing in that partic- on that particular

00:13:33.806 --> 00:13:35.555
page for that particular reader.

00:13:36.289 --> 00:13:40.884
Uh, that's, that's not a bug that
gets fixed with a better model.

00:13:41.247 --> 00:13:45.464
It's a structural sort of limitation,
uh, of generating a description

00:13:45.689 --> 00:13:49.069
from an image alone with no page
context and no understanding

00:13:49.175 --> 00:13:50.956
of the intent, of that intent.

00:13:51.489 --> 00:13:55.997
And there are places it doesn't just
underperform, it fails outright.

00:13:56.406 --> 00:13:59.018
So you have text inside images, flyers.

00:13:59.020 --> 00:14:00.665
We see this all the
time at the university.

00:14:00.723 --> 00:14:05.276
Text, uh, inside flyers,
infographics, screenshots.

00:14:05.661 --> 00:14:09.212
They get summarized or dropped
instead of transcribed, so the

00:14:09.212 --> 00:14:15.548
actual information a sighted user,
uh, sort of would read directly just

00:14:15.549 --> 00:14:17.213
disappears for a screen reader user.

00:14:18.227 --> 00:14:24.168
Charts and data visualizations,
they get a generic label, uh, like,

00:14:24.452 --> 00:14:30.250
quote, "a graph," when a sighted user
glances at that same s- uh, chart and

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:35.699
immediately sees the trend, the outlier,
uh, the thing the chart was meant

00:14:35.708 --> 00:14:37.774
to build, uh, and, and communicate.

00:14:40.670 --> 00:14:45.582
Decorative images that should have empty
alt text, we're all familiar with alt

00:14:45.585 --> 00:14:50.994
equals quote quote, uh, so that a screen
reader, uh, skips past them entirely,

00:14:51.189 --> 00:14:56.119
instead get a sort of full confident
sentence describing them, which adds

00:14:56.119 --> 00:14:58.314
noise to every single page they appear on.

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:03.880
And anything culturally or contextually
specific, a person, a campus

00:15:03.882 --> 00:15:08.280
building, a logo, that gets mislabeled
with total unearned confidence.

00:15:08.647 --> 00:15:11.193
That confidence is the dangerous
part of all this, by the way.

00:15:11.596 --> 00:15:17.177
So nobody flags a wrong caption for
review if it reads like a correct one.

00:15:17.569 --> 00:15:22.414
A human writing bad alt text usually
writes something that sounds, mm,

00:15:22.462 --> 00:15:27.792
kind of uncertain or incomplete,
or sometimes eyebrows go up.

00:15:28.159 --> 00:15:31.332
But a model writing bad alt
text sounds exactly as confident

00:15:31.364 --> 00:15:32.843
as it does when it's right.

00:15:33.227 --> 00:15:36.386
So we should all sit with that
and think about that for a moment.

00:15:37.306 --> 00:15:38.585
So where does it actually help?

00:15:39.582 --> 00:15:45.023
As a first draft, never a final answer,
and I want that distinction doing the

00:15:45.023 --> 00:15:48.700
real work in your head, not just as
a caveat I'm saying to sound balanced

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:50.062
or like I know what I'm talking about.

00:15:50.549 --> 00:15:57.500
It's genuinely useful for triage,
telling you which of your 40,000 images,

00:15:57.501 --> 00:16:02.111
that's a bit of an exaggeration, but
40,000 images have zero alt text at all.

00:16:02.617 --> 00:16:07.760
So a human knows where to start
prioritizing instead of scrolling a media

00:16:07.761 --> 00:16:11.390
library, um, blind, no pun intended.

00:16:11.846 --> 00:16:16.855
It's a reasonable starting, uh,
point for straightforward photos, um,

00:16:16.888 --> 00:16:21.593
that a content editor then edits for
purpose before it ever gets published.

00:16:21.924 --> 00:16:24.369
Uh, not something that you
just accept and move on from.

00:16:24.903 --> 00:16:30.057
It's useful when you need speed on
volume for the accuracy, uh, the

00:16:30.058 --> 00:16:34.044
accuracy bar is genuinely low, uh,
which is not your homepage, and it is

00:16:34.047 --> 00:16:38.915
not anything that's driving a decision
a user is trying to make on your site.

00:16:39.366 --> 00:16:42.780
And I should add, it's also,
um, you know, if you're being

00:16:42.788 --> 00:16:44.919
actively sued, I wouldn't do that.

00:16:45.538 --> 00:16:49.473
Used this way, in that narrow
lane, it saves real time.

00:16:50.358 --> 00:16:53.828
Used outside that lane, it creates
the failures we just walked through.

00:16:54.465 --> 00:16:54.891
All right.

00:16:54.895 --> 00:17:00.255
So now we're gonna talk about scanning
tools and overlays, um, which have

00:17:00.255 --> 00:17:01.852
been all the rage the last few years.

00:17:02.052 --> 00:17:04.782
What are the automated scanners are
they- what are they actually good at?

00:17:05.843 --> 00:17:10.964
I do want to give scanning tools, uh,
their sort of, uh, their due before

00:17:10.964 --> 00:17:16.558
I, uh, what they're sort of due before
I tear into them because- Um, they're

00:17:16.558 --> 00:17:21.173
genuinely good at a specific, well
sort of, uh, defined category of

00:17:21.174 --> 00:17:26.015
thing, and I don't want this sort of
section to read as dismissive of tools

00:17:26.092 --> 00:17:29.892
that a lot of you are using well,
and we are using some of those tools.

00:17:29.919 --> 00:17:32.441
Um, so I do think that's worth mentioning.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:34.676
Missing alt attributes in the markup.

00:17:35.003 --> 00:17:38.812
Color contrast ratio against,
uh, WCAG thresholds, right?

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:39.873
These sorts of things.

00:17:39.873 --> 00:17:40.895
Missing form labels.

00:17:41.188 --> 00:17:43.128
Uh, empty link text and buttons.

00:17:43.154 --> 00:17:44.806
Missing, uh, document language.

00:17:44.808 --> 00:17:46.037
Duplicate IDs.

00:17:46.477 --> 00:17:49.312
Basic HTML structure pr- uh, problems.

00:17:49.743 --> 00:17:51.393
Even your occasional ARIA markup.

00:17:52.025 --> 00:17:56.356
These are real code-level
machine-checkable issues, and scanners

00:17:56.361 --> 00:18:01.004
catch them fast and reliably, far faster
than a human doing the same check by hand.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:04.027
So that's a real contribution
to a real workflow, sure.

00:18:04.435 --> 00:18:07.331
Keep that in mind, because the next
few slides are about what happens

00:18:07.344 --> 00:18:12.071
when that category, which is genuinely
useful, gets treat- uh, treated as

00:18:12.072 --> 00:18:16.524
the whole job instead of the one slice
of the job it actually s- um, covers.

00:18:17.662 --> 00:18:20.831
So here's the volume problem, and
this is one people underestimate

00:18:20.831 --> 00:18:22.500
until they've lived through it.

00:18:22.747 --> 00:18:28.149
So you run a scan against a large,
uh, decentralized university site.

00:18:28.209 --> 00:18:33.802
We have over 550 for o- excuse
me, four-- 550 for ours, and

00:18:33.802 --> 00:18:35.366
that's just as of this recording.

00:18:35.599 --> 00:18:39.384
Think about a large decentralized
university site, and you can get thousands

00:18:39.385 --> 00:18:41.617
of flagged issues in a single pass.

00:18:42.022 --> 00:18:44.531
A handful of those are
genuinely high impact.

00:18:45.107 --> 00:18:48.552
So think about a broken form,
a keyboard trap inside a modal,

00:18:48.969 --> 00:18:51.383
something that actually blocks a task.

00:18:51.811 --> 00:18:54.072
A huge share of that
are low-value repeats.

00:18:54.329 --> 00:19:00.004
The same missing alt attribute flagged
900 times because it's baked into a

00:19:00.005 --> 00:19:01.945
template used across the whole site.

00:19:02.460 --> 00:19:09.443
So one root cause, uh, sort of
becomes 900 line items on a report.

00:19:10.355 --> 00:19:13.312
So what actually happens on the
ground is the team looking at that

00:19:13.312 --> 00:19:17.454
report doesn't triage it because
triaging a spreadsheet with 2,000

00:19:17.455 --> 00:19:22.079
rows is its own full-time job on top
of everyone's existing full-time job.

00:19:22.673 --> 00:19:26.053
Uh, I will pause here briefly and say
that w- some of our content editors,

00:19:26.055 --> 00:19:30.092
you know, managing, uh, a department
website or what have you, is like maybe,

00:19:30.092 --> 00:19:33.476
I'm, I'm sorta spitballing here, two
to five percent of their actual job.

00:19:33.936 --> 00:19:37.687
And I'm sure many of you in the higher
ed space listening to this can relate,

00:19:38.018 --> 00:19:43.030
um, specifically in higher ed, where
we're asked, you know, to do a, a

00:19:43.030 --> 00:19:45.370
lot w- often on very little, right?

00:19:45.371 --> 00:19:49.754
So they drown in the noise, and
functionally, they prioritize

00:19:49.754 --> 00:19:53.074
nothing because there's no
real obvious place to start.

00:19:53.443 --> 00:19:58.483
So a tool that surfaces 2,000 things
and helped you act on effectively

00:19:58.493 --> 00:20:03.291
zero of them has not helped you, even
though the report itself looks thorough

00:20:03.291 --> 00:20:05.008
and looks like progress was measured.

00:20:05.743 --> 00:20:07.053
So accessibility overlays.

00:20:07.503 --> 00:20:09.744
Um, this is the promise
versus the pattern.

00:20:10.298 --> 00:20:13.945
So now the overlay conversation
specifically because this deserves its

00:20:13.946 --> 00:20:17.008
own slide and its own moment of clarity.

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:20.242
We're all pretty aligned in the
accessibility, uh, space about these,

00:20:20.245 --> 00:20:22.273
but it does bear, uh, repeating anyway.

00:20:22.692 --> 00:20:27.105
So the promise is one line of JavaScript,
for example, an overlay widget that

00:20:27.106 --> 00:20:32.406
scans the page in the browser and
fixes accessibility issues live- No

00:20:32.406 --> 00:20:37.116
development work required, no code
review, no waiting on an engineer sprint.

00:20:37.645 --> 00:20:42.059
So that's the entire pitch, and it's
an easy one to say yes to when you have

00:20:42.061 --> 00:20:44.148
no budget and a compliance deadline.

00:20:44.192 --> 00:20:45.697
Remember when we talked
about getting sued?

00:20:46.845 --> 00:20:51.755
The pattern, as this has been documented
extensively across the accessibility

00:20:51.757 --> 00:20:56.168
community for years now, is that
overlays can adjust sort of level things.

00:20:56.439 --> 00:21:00.034
Contrast, font size, maybe
a reading mode toggle.

00:21:00.456 --> 00:21:07.274
They cannot fix excess, uh, keyboard
traps, um, missing semantic structure, or

00:21:07.274 --> 00:21:11.677
broken sort of focus order because those
problems live in the underlying markup.

00:21:12.180 --> 00:21:15.232
And an overlay by design is
not touching your markup.

00:21:15.637 --> 00:21:19.071
It's layering something on
top of it in the browser.

00:21:19.700 --> 00:21:24.120
So screen reader users, including me, as I
mentioned, routinely report that overlays

00:21:24.472 --> 00:21:30.534
make a site harder to use, not easier,
because n- now there's a widget actively

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:34.712
fighting with our assistive technology
on top of whatever accessibility

00:21:34.915 --> 00:21:36.946
problems were already underneath it.

00:21:37.656 --> 00:21:40.514
So this is where automated
scanning does real damage.

00:21:40.825 --> 00:21:44.586
And I want to be precise about the
mechanics because it's not that

00:21:44.587 --> 00:21:48.341
the tool is wrong, it's that the
tool is confidently incomplete.

00:21:48.918 --> 00:21:49.859
So what do I mean by that?

00:21:51.025 --> 00:21:54.413
So you get a scan, it comes
back clean, contrast passes, alt

00:21:54.418 --> 00:21:57.273
attributes, um, are-- they're present.

00:21:57.724 --> 00:22:02.698
The dashboard is green across the
board, and someone writes, "WCAG 2.1

00:22:02.701 --> 00:22:07.217
AA compliant" into a VPAT, that's your
voluntary product accessibility template.

00:22:07.217 --> 00:22:11.292
It's actually the accessibility
conformance report when it's done in your

00:22:11.293 --> 00:22:15.926
ACR, and moves on with their day because
the dashboard told them it was fine.

00:22:17.337 --> 00:22:18.698
So here's the actual problem.

00:22:19.475 --> 00:22:24.770
Automated tools can only reliably check a
fraction of WCAG success criteria, roughly

00:22:25.036 --> 00:22:27.419
a third by most credible estimates.

00:22:27.514 --> 00:22:29.264
You can Google it and do your homework.

00:22:29.266 --> 00:22:33.654
Don't trust me, um, I'm just
reporting the data and what they say.

00:22:34.116 --> 00:22:37.686
Because the rest genuinely require
human judgment to evaluate.

00:22:38.253 --> 00:22:42.141
So a clean scan tells you nothing
about whether a keyboard-only user can

00:22:42.141 --> 00:22:46.461
actually get through your checkout flow,
whether your modal traps focus when it

00:22:46.461 --> 00:22:52.150
opens and returns it correctly when it
closes, or whether the page makes any

00:22:52.474 --> 00:22:56.737
sort of coherent sense r- uh, reading
it top to bottom with a screen reader.

00:22:56.746 --> 00:22:58.671
Screen readers read top
to bottom, left to right.

00:22:59.087 --> 00:23:02.762
Um, and so that's very
important to keep in mind.

00:23:03.216 --> 00:23:08.183
That green dashboard is not evidence
that your site is accessible.

00:23:09.070 --> 00:23:12.000
It's really just evidence that nobody
checked the other two-thirds of the

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:16.691
standard, and a green dashboard that
gets treated as a sort of finished

00:23:16.701 --> 00:23:22.134
checklist is worse than no dashboard
at all because it actively discourages

00:23:22.135 --> 00:23:23.396
anyone from looking further.

00:23:24.064 --> 00:23:27.310
So now we'll talk about
plugin-based fixes, uh, move into

00:23:27.311 --> 00:23:28.681
the Gutenberg sort of content.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:29.276
Next slide.

00:23:29.786 --> 00:23:34.097
So the third area, plugin-based
automated fixes, and this is

00:23:34.099 --> 00:23:36.394
one very specific to WordPress.

00:23:36.487 --> 00:23:37.695
You came for a WordPress talk.

00:23:37.979 --> 00:23:43.721
Very specific to WordPress as a platform,
uh, which is why it belongs in a talk

00:23:43.736 --> 00:23:45.600
aimed at this audience specifically.

00:23:46.019 --> 00:23:49.973
So the plugin ecosystem is WordPress's
biggest strength, I will start

00:23:49.973 --> 00:23:54.301
with that, but also its biggest
accessibility liability at the same time.

00:23:54.522 --> 00:23:56.911
And both of these things are
true for the same reason.

00:23:57.191 --> 00:23:58.844
Anyone can build and ship one.

00:23:59.523 --> 00:24:03.854
So you search accessibility in the plugin
repository, and you get everything from

00:24:03.861 --> 00:24:09.539
genuinely useful linting tools that flag
issues for a developer to review, all

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:15.313
the way to AI-driven widgets promising
automatic remediation across your entire

00:24:15.315 --> 00:24:17.773
site with zero code, uh, development work.

00:24:18.401 --> 00:24:21.171
And Gutenberg makes this
worse, not better, because of

00:24:21.171 --> 00:24:22.862
block patterns specifically.

00:24:23.252 --> 00:24:29.152
So one inaccessible block gets built
once by one developer under one deadline,

00:24:29.813 --> 00:24:34.331
but then it gets reused across hundreds
of pages by content editors who have

00:24:34.331 --> 00:24:38.349
no idea it was never tested with a
screen reader in, in the first place.

00:24:38.365 --> 00:24:40.093
Also, the testing isn't their job.

00:24:40.099 --> 00:24:43.100
They're just trying to put it
on the webpage and doing what

00:24:43.101 --> 00:24:44.267
they are being asked to do.

00:24:45.313 --> 00:24:49.342
Because from their side, it just looks
like a normal block in the inserter.

00:24:50.195 --> 00:24:53.369
A single mistake doesn't stay a
single mistake in this environment.

00:24:54.336 --> 00:24:58.410
It replicates every time that someone
drags that block onto a new page.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:00.100
Uh, next slide

00:25:03.057 --> 00:25:06.869
All right, so let's be very
specific about the actual boundary.

00:25:06.936 --> 00:25:10.981
Um, this is the part that matters
most for your own decision, uh,

00:25:11.013 --> 00:25:12.604
this week, not just for this talk.

00:25:13.158 --> 00:25:18.879
So plug-ins can genuinely fix things like
missing alt attributes on legacy images,

00:25:18.908 --> 00:25:26.246
color contrast values in a design system,
uh, a missing lang attribute, and they can

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:31.928
auto-generate, uh, basic ARIA landmarks
on a standard predictable template

00:25:32.299 --> 00:25:34.095
where the structure is known in advance.

00:25:34.477 --> 00:25:35.455
So that's real value.

00:25:36.133 --> 00:25:39.778
I don't want, uh, you to sort of
walk out of here dismissing plug-ins

00:25:39.792 --> 00:25:42.686
wholesale because that would just,
uh, that would be just as wrong,

00:25:42.692 --> 00:25:44.474
frankly, as trusting them wholesale.

00:25:45.664 --> 00:25:49.829
So what they cannot touch is anything that
requires sort of understanding intent.

00:25:50.858 --> 00:25:55.629
Whether the focus orders through a custom
interaction actually makes sense to

00:25:55.629 --> 00:26:02.212
someone navigating it sequentially, um,
whether a modal genuinely traps focus

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:06.822
while it's open and returns it, uh, uh,
to the trigger element when it closes.

00:26:06.848 --> 00:26:08.227
Getting a little bit into the weeds here.

00:26:08.227 --> 00:26:13.080
But, uh, cognitive load and whether the
sort of reading sequence makes sense as

00:26:13.081 --> 00:26:20.441
a sort of coherent narrative rather than
a jumble of, uh, DOM order, and whether

00:26:20.442 --> 00:26:25.155
the, uh, pattern itself is coherent
to someone navigating with a screen

00:26:25.155 --> 00:26:27.159
reader instead of a mouse and a monitor.

00:26:27.916 --> 00:26:32.799
So those all require human, a human who
understands that interface is actually,

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:36.215
uh, what that interface is trying to
accomplish for the person using it,

00:26:36.458 --> 00:26:40.762
and no plug-in, however well-built,
is doing that reasoning for you.

00:26:41.969 --> 00:26:42.876
All right, next slide.

00:26:44.222 --> 00:26:47.418
Here's the pattern that I, uh,
run into constantly in audits,

00:26:47.819 --> 00:26:51.779
and I want to be clear that I'm
generalizing across dozens of specific

00:26:51.801 --> 00:26:53.611
tickets over y- uh, several years.

00:26:53.940 --> 00:26:57.447
Uh, I'm not describing one incident
I happened to remember for this talk.

00:26:57.895 --> 00:27:04.103
So a plugin ships an accessible accordion
or tab component, and I'm putting

00:27:04.105 --> 00:27:06.181
"accessible" in quotes deliberately.

00:27:07.264 --> 00:27:09.106
It passes an automated scan clean.

00:27:09.117 --> 00:27:11.153
The markup looks correct on paper.

00:27:11.579 --> 00:27:15.225
ARIA roles are technically present
where the checklist expects

00:27:15.226 --> 00:27:17.421
them, and according to the spec.

00:27:19.251 --> 00:27:23.211
It gets reused across dozens of pages
through a block pattern because that's

00:27:23.213 --> 00:27:24.928
the entire point of block patterns.

00:27:25.617 --> 00:27:27.429
Reuse at scale, right?

00:27:28.013 --> 00:27:32.693
Then a real screen reader user hits
it in the wild, and focus never

00:27:32.694 --> 00:27:36.849
actually lands inside the expanded
panel when they activate it.

00:27:37.264 --> 00:27:40.329
The content is sitting
right there on the page.

00:27:40.851 --> 00:27:45.241
It is completely unreachable in the
actual navigation order a screen

00:27:45.241 --> 00:27:48.303
reader, uh, user is-- experiences.

00:27:48.706 --> 00:27:52.866
The automated scan passed, uh, the
plugin's marketing claimed accessibility,

00:27:52.879 --> 00:27:58.620
and a real person using the site the
way it is actually used every day could

00:27:58.620 --> 00:28:00.480
not get to the content they came for.

00:28:00.899 --> 00:28:05.572
So that's really the whole gap that
this-- we're, we're covering here.

00:28:06.003 --> 00:28:07.369
Just one concrete example.

00:28:07.708 --> 00:28:09.114
Why this keeps on happening.

00:28:09.916 --> 00:28:14.234
So why does this keep happening
when the failure modes I just walked

00:28:14.234 --> 00:28:18.822
through are well documented and not
exactly a secret in this community?

00:28:19.213 --> 00:28:20.473
Here's the answer underneath the pattern.

00:28:20.702 --> 00:28:24.668
When alt text was written
manually, one content editor

00:28:24.702 --> 00:28:26.687
owned the outcome, full stop.

00:28:27.338 --> 00:28:31.113
If it was wrong, there was exactly
one person to talk to about fixing it.

00:28:31.402 --> 00:28:36.014
Now, the description came from a vendor's
AI model, the scan came from a different

00:28:36.014 --> 00:28:41.235
vendor's, uh, crawler, and the plugin
was built by a third party nobody on your

00:28:41.236 --> 00:28:48.322
team has ever had a conversation with,
let alone, um, a contract, um, with,

00:28:48.497 --> 00:28:53.291
uh, that sort of specifies what happens
when their tool gets something wrong.

00:28:54.114 --> 00:28:55.583
We could have a whole
talk about procurement.

00:28:56.569 --> 00:29:01.811
Ask after something breaks in production,
"Who's actually responsible?" And you

00:29:01.813 --> 00:29:04.936
get three d- uh, three different vendors
pointing in three different directions

00:29:05.198 --> 00:29:09.750
and zero people internally who s- will
say plainly that it was theirs to catch.

00:29:10.110 --> 00:29:13.897
So that diffusion isn't really
an accident of how these tools

00:29:13.905 --> 00:29:15.826
happened to develop independently.

00:29:15.985 --> 00:29:19.255
It is functionally really the
business model, unfortunately.

00:29:20.006 --> 00:29:24.730
Nobody selling you an a- an automated
fix wants to be the one who's holding

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:28.488
the liability when it doesn't work,
and the contract structure around

00:29:28.779 --> 00:29:30.185
most of these tools reflects that.

00:29:30.850 --> 00:29:31.747
All right, next slide.

00:29:32.525 --> 00:29:37.178
And there's a second quieter cost
here, and it's- About the humans in

00:29:37.178 --> 00:29:39.351
the workflow, not the tools themselves.

00:29:39.673 --> 00:29:45.849
So content editors who used to have
to understand why an image needed alt

00:29:45.858 --> 00:29:50.199
text, what actually made a description
useful to someone who couldn't see

00:29:50.199 --> 00:29:56.400
the image, now they click accept
on an AI sort of suggestion and

00:29:56.812 --> 00:29:58.880
move straight to the next upload.

00:29:59.630 --> 00:30:04.074
Developers who used to reason through
focus, uh, through focus order themselves,

00:30:04.372 --> 00:30:09.168
tabbing through an interface by hand,
now they trust a linter's green check

00:30:09.168 --> 00:30:11.312
mark and move to the next ticket.

00:30:12.264 --> 00:30:16.097
That skill doesn't vanish
the moment it gets automated.

00:30:16.592 --> 00:30:20.521
It vanishes gradually, right,
because it stops getting practice.

00:30:20.870 --> 00:30:22.769
Atrophy, if you, if you like.

00:30:23.101 --> 00:30:28.012
The same way any skill r-
does without repetition.

00:30:29.308 --> 00:30:35.003
And the problem with that erosion is
really it's not there the one time a year

00:30:35.231 --> 00:30:39.582
the automation genuinely can't handle
the situation in front of it, which

00:30:39.677 --> 00:30:43.617
based on everything we've kinda walked
through in this talk, is meaningfully more

00:30:43.617 --> 00:30:48.831
often than the, uh, than the marketing
for these tools would have you believe.

00:30:49.208 --> 00:30:52.866
So you end up with a team who has
lost the muscle right around the

00:30:52.886 --> 00:30:55.135
time it needed, they needed it most.

00:30:55.415 --> 00:30:56.506
Um, next slide.

00:30:57.193 --> 00:31:02.888
And underneath all that- This was never
really sort of a technology problem.

00:31:02.938 --> 00:31:06.678
If you, if, uh, if you've done
your homework, WCAG ha- uh, the

00:31:06.679 --> 00:31:11.832
standard, um, for mo- for websites,
at least, um, the gold standard has

00:31:11.833 --> 00:31:14.071
really been around since, uh, 1999.

00:31:14.326 --> 00:31:16.724
Uh, it's gone through a few
iterations, but the point still stands.

00:31:16.729 --> 00:31:18.578
It's been around for over
a quarter of a century.

00:31:20.008 --> 00:31:24.460
It's an incentive problem, and I think
naming that out loud is more useful

00:31:24.461 --> 00:31:27.833
than pretending the next model, uh,
version of the model will fix it.

00:31:28.364 --> 00:31:32.736
AI accessibility tools get sort
of funded and shipped fast because

00:31:32.736 --> 00:31:34.471
they solve a business problem first.

00:31:34.757 --> 00:31:38.661
Liability exposure, speed to
market, head count you don't have

00:31:38.663 --> 00:31:43.224
to hire in a budget cycle where
head count is hard to get approved.

00:31:43.451 --> 00:31:49.202
Disabled users are not getting, um,
sort of faster attention because a, an

00:31:49.203 --> 00:31:54.473
organization, um, sat down and really
decided accessibility finally mattered

00:31:54.473 --> 00:31:56.786
enough to prioritize it on its own merits.

00:31:57.865 --> 00:32:01.198
We, as a disabled people, are
getting attention as a byproduct

00:32:01.199 --> 00:32:05.431
of somebody else's more fundable
priority, and that is the truth, and

00:32:05.432 --> 00:32:09.831
that's worth being honest about, um,
rather than trying to be flattering.

00:32:10.679 --> 00:32:14.559
That's genuinely, uh, fine when
the byproduct actually works

00:32:14.581 --> 00:32:16.158
and holds up under real use.

00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:20.808
It stops being fine the moment we bought
out to the tool, uh, bought out the

00:32:20.808 --> 00:32:24.764
tool and that quietly becomes really
the entire organization's definition

00:32:24.765 --> 00:32:26.762
of, "We handled accessibility."

00:32:27.095 --> 00:32:31.694
Because that's exactly the moment
nobody is checking anymore, and the

00:32:31.695 --> 00:32:37.436
checking is part of actually really
what protects your users All right,

00:32:37.437 --> 00:32:39.131
so we're getting into the guardrails.

00:32:39.497 --> 00:32:41.165
Um, we have about 10 minutes left.

00:32:41.195 --> 00:32:43.735
So, uh, next slide, 27.

00:32:44.323 --> 00:32:45.560
All right, so let's get practical.

00:32:45.916 --> 00:32:47.250
I've told you all what's wrong with it.

00:32:47.512 --> 00:32:47.849
Great.

00:32:48.718 --> 00:32:51.957
Five guardrails, and every one of
these is something you can start using

00:32:51.957 --> 00:32:56.738
next week without ripping anything
out of your existing, um, sorta stack.

00:32:57.127 --> 00:32:59.937
So number one, AI produces drafts.

00:33:00.253 --> 00:33:01.344
Humans make decisions.

00:33:01.762 --> 00:33:05.830
Every single AI output in your
accessibility workflow, generated alt

00:33:05.840 --> 00:33:12.221
text, a scan result, plug-in generated
markup, gets a named human who reviews

00:33:12.222 --> 00:33:13.750
it before it ships to production.

00:33:14.334 --> 00:33:19.707
Not a policy d-- uh, sort of document
somewhere in some place, nobody

00:33:19.707 --> 00:33:24.487
knows where it is, that says, "Review
happens." An actual specific person

00:33:24.680 --> 00:33:29.209
in the actual workflow whose sign-off
is required before it goes live.

00:33:29.556 --> 00:33:35.392
And here's the test I'd want you to take
home, um, from this slide particularly.

00:33:35.750 --> 00:33:40.543
If you can't name right now off the
top of your head exactly who reviews

00:33:40.545 --> 00:33:45.015
the AI's output on your team, the
honest answer is that nobody does.

00:33:46.035 --> 00:33:50.327
And that's something worth fixing before
you add another automated tool on top

00:33:50.328 --> 00:33:52.529
of the ones you already have unreviewed.

00:33:53.195 --> 00:33:59.193
Guardrail number two: real assistive
technology testing stays non-negotiable.

00:34:00.378 --> 00:34:03.513
No exceptions carved
out for tight deadlines.

00:34:04.004 --> 00:34:08.434
A clean automated scan is not a
substitute for an actual person

00:34:08.445 --> 00:34:13.449
using a screen reader, keyboard-only
navigation, um, or voice control,

00:34:13.449 --> 00:34:17.086
which is something that often doesn't
get checked, um, voice control, to

00:34:17.087 --> 00:34:18.871
get through your critical workflows.

00:34:19.153 --> 00:34:20.475
And what are those critical workflows?

00:34:20.488 --> 00:34:21.532
We keep talking about that.

00:34:21.846 --> 00:34:25.211
Things like checkout,
sign-up, contact forms.

00:34:25.401 --> 00:34:28.264
Whatever your users are actually
on your site to accomplish.

00:34:29.135 --> 00:34:33.629
Register for classes,
admissions, apply, whatever.

00:34:34.706 --> 00:34:37.882
Automated tools reliably catch,
roughly, like I said, about a third

00:34:37.886 --> 00:34:43.308
of WCAG success criteria, which we
established a little bit ago, right?

00:34:43.501 --> 00:34:48.933
So as I said, if you know math, that
means the other two-thirds, they really

00:34:48.933 --> 00:34:53.627
do require, uh, human evaluating directly
on a regular, uh, recurring schedule.

00:34:54.178 --> 00:34:58.459
So you've actually budgeted for it,
not reactively the first time you hear

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:02.793
about a problem from a support ticket,
a complaint, or worse, like we said,

00:35:02.799 --> 00:35:07.407
a demand letter that turns a workflow
into a legal want-- uh, legal problem.

00:35:07.644 --> 00:35:07.897
All right.

00:35:07.911 --> 00:35:08.412
Next slide.

00:35:09.513 --> 00:35:11.235
And g-guardrail number three.

00:35:12.638 --> 00:35:15.542
Interrogate the vendor before you
install the plugin or sign the

00:35:15.544 --> 00:35:17.582
contract, not after something breaks.

00:35:17.949 --> 00:35:20.395
Four questions I'd want
answered every single time.

00:35:20.978 --> 00:35:21.603
That's okay.

00:35:21.881 --> 00:35:22.475
Take notes.

00:35:22.881 --> 00:35:25.279
What training data was
this model built on?

00:35:25.625 --> 00:35:30.062
And can anyone outside the company
actually verify that claim independently?

00:35:30.915 --> 00:35:34.674
What is their published false
positive and false negative rate,

00:35:35.020 --> 00:35:36.908
and how did they measure it?

00:35:37.124 --> 00:35:40.111
Not what the marketing page
asserts without evidence.

00:35:40.826 --> 00:35:44.799
They won't be super thrilled that
you're asking these, but they are worth

00:35:44.801 --> 00:35:48.194
asking because most will not ask these.

00:35:48.982 --> 00:35:52.696
Has this actually been evaluated by
disabled users performing real tasks,

00:35:53.118 --> 00:35:56.955
or only by an internal QA team that
already knows what the tool is supposed

00:35:56.964 --> 00:35:59.821
to do and isn't a representative test?

00:36:00.491 --> 00:36:02.307
And they should be able
to do it on a live demo.

00:36:03.721 --> 00:36:07.732
And what happens contractually
in writing when it gets something

00:36:07.732 --> 00:36:09.609
wrong on your production site?

00:36:10.558 --> 00:36:12.308
Who bears that cost?

00:36:13.263 --> 00:36:17.634
If a vendor can't answer these four
questions clearly, um, that reluctance

00:36:17.656 --> 00:36:21.286
is itself an answer, and that's the
one you should weigh most heavily.

00:36:22.031 --> 00:36:26.729
Guardrail four, track
outcomes, not scan scores.

00:36:27.151 --> 00:36:33.282
It is very easy to report internally that
you reduced flagged issues from 4,000 to

00:36:33.284 --> 00:36:38.527
200 and present it as measurable progress
in a quarterly, uh, sort of review.

00:36:38.919 --> 00:36:42.024
That number on its own says
really absolutely nothing

00:36:42.037 --> 00:36:45.618
about whether a real user could
complete a real task on your site.

00:36:46.268 --> 00:36:50.149
The metric that actually means something
that looks closer to this, a screen

00:36:50.149 --> 00:36:54.786
reader user completed checkout unassisted
in roughly the same number of steps as

00:36:54.786 --> 00:36:56.958
a sighted user did on the same flow.

00:36:57.360 --> 00:36:59.813
That's harder to measure
than a scan score.

00:37:00.232 --> 00:37:03.888
Uh, it takes actual sort of usability
testing with actual assistive

00:37:03.888 --> 00:37:07.586
technology users, and it is the
only version of this that tells you

00:37:07.586 --> 00:37:12.211
whether you improved something that
touches a person's actual sort of

00:37:12.211 --> 00:37:16.190
life instead of improving a dashboard
that only your team ever looks at.

00:37:16.882 --> 00:37:21.981
And guardrail five, keep the thinking in
the workflow deliberately and by design.

00:37:22.395 --> 00:37:27.404
Don't let sort of accept AI
suggestion become a pure reflex

00:37:27.770 --> 00:37:29.607
that nobody actually engages with.

00:37:29.912 --> 00:37:35.516
You wanna build in one small intentional
point of friction where a sort of

00:37:35.518 --> 00:37:39.509
content editor, they have to write a
single sentence of reasoning and explain

00:37:39.511 --> 00:37:43.641
why this alt text actually works with
this specific page and this specific

00:37:43.641 --> 00:37:46.029
purpose, not just that it was generated.

00:37:46.461 --> 00:37:50.372
That one sentence is enough to keep
the underlying skill really sort

00:37:50.373 --> 00:37:54.468
of alive in the people actually
doing the work, um, day to day.

00:37:54.795 --> 00:37:59.736
And that one sentence is the sort of
difference between a workflow that sort

00:37:59.736 --> 00:38:04.131
of quietly degrades over two years because
nobody on the team is actually thinking

00:38:04.415 --> 00:38:06.731
about sort of accessibility anymore.

00:38:06.993 --> 00:38:10.823
They're just clicking accept, and a
workflow that holds up, uh, pressure

00:38:10.826 --> 00:38:15.317
because, uh, thinking never left
the process in the first place.

00:38:16.446 --> 00:38:18.754
Finally, we'll bring this home.

00:38:18.998 --> 00:38:22.832
Three things I want you to really look out
for when you walk out of the room with.

00:38:23.290 --> 00:38:27.931
One, you can now identify the actual
failure modes we covered, right?

00:38:27.933 --> 00:38:31.884
So that's with the, uh,
items that we talked about.

00:38:32.455 --> 00:38:38.736
And you can also tell the difference, uh,
between AI use that genuinely supports

00:38:38.745 --> 00:38:44.006
your work and AI that just manufactures
false confidence on a dashboard.

00:38:44.696 --> 00:38:46.743
It's a draft generator and a triage tool.

00:38:47.131 --> 00:38:51.614
Remember, it is not a decision-maker, and
it is never a substitute for an actual

00:38:51.614 --> 00:38:57.386
disabled person using your site, uh, sort
of the way that it really should be used.

00:38:57.397 --> 00:39:03.013
And three, you have, uh, sort of five
concrete guardrails you can start applying

00:39:03.015 --> 00:39:06.013
this week, not a five-year roadmap.

00:39:06.129 --> 00:39:08.439
That requires a budget approval cycle.

00:39:09.028 --> 00:39:10.749
Starting Monday morning, if you want.

00:39:11.330 --> 00:39:14.864
Remember, you didn't fix accessibility,
you automated the problem, unless

00:39:14.864 --> 00:39:18.734
a human is still doing the thinking
somewhere in that pipeline.

00:39:19.004 --> 00:39:20.616
So these tools are not going away.

00:39:20.917 --> 00:39:21.821
Let's make that clear.

00:39:21.821 --> 00:39:25.421
And honestly, they shouldn't because
some of what they do is genuinely

00:39:25.422 --> 00:39:31.038
useful when it's scoped correctly, which
is exactly what we spent, uh, really

00:39:31.041 --> 00:39:32.487
the last 40 minutes walking through.

00:39:33.158 --> 00:39:36.477
Um, but useful is not the same
thing as sufficient, and fast

00:39:36.477 --> 00:39:37.895
is not the same as correct.

00:39:38.484 --> 00:39:41.705
That's really the whole, uh, what
I want you to take away from this.

00:39:41.993 --> 00:39:43.355
Uh, thank you so much for having me.

00:39:43.685 --> 00:39:46.913
I'm around for the question and
answer session, and I'm genuinely

00:39:46.915 --> 00:39:50.731
happy to get into the weeds on
any of these with anyone who wants

00:39:50.732 --> 00:39:52.381
to talk, uh, specifics afterward.

00:39:52.663 --> 00:39:53.013
Thank you.

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:58.928
Abigail: Thank you so much
for your presentation.

00:39:58.929 --> 00:40:03.471
I gotta say, the chat, the
Q&A just exploded as- Yes,

00:40:03.471 --> 00:40:04.529
it did … you were speaking.

00:40:05.095 --> 00:40:09.695
And I also wanna key in on something
as we're just thanking you for

00:40:09.708 --> 00:40:11.945
giving this phenomenal presentation.

00:40:11.945 --> 00:40:16.460
I did see somebody post in the
chat, "Kevin, this presentation is

00:40:16.513 --> 00:40:21.023
so good that I'm actively plotting
