00:40:35.338 --> 00:40:37.882
gonna go ahead and get right to them.

00:40:37.882 --> 00:40:43.646
And Kevin, I'm gonna give you, uh, what I
think is maybe a, a rather easier question

00:40:43.647 --> 00:40:47.742
to answer, um, to start out our Q&A.

00:40:47.758 --> 00:40:53.598
Um, not necessarily AI generated, but,
hey, are there any recommended guidelines

00:40:53.638 --> 00:41:00.074
for judging whether images should be
considered decorative or informational?

00:41:01.144 --> 00:41:03.073
Kevin: Um, that's a good one.

00:41:03.421 --> 00:41:05.116
I would say… You can
all hear me, I hope.

00:41:05.152 --> 00:41:05.504
Yes.

00:41:06.205 --> 00:41:07.197
Um, yeah.

00:41:07.429 --> 00:41:14.639
I would say think about is it contributing
to somebody's understanding of the page?

00:41:15.025 --> 00:41:19.313
Uh, in other words, is it just,
you know, back in the '90s or

00:41:19.313 --> 00:41:22.200
2000s we had graphic spacer, you
know, and everybody knows them.

00:41:22.313 --> 00:41:24.153
Maybe some people are too young
to know what I'm talking about.

00:41:24.511 --> 00:41:32.575
Um, or is it, you know, university mascot,
um, smiles at student, like whatever.

00:41:32.593 --> 00:41:38.574
Like, would my life be better off
if I knew it existed or, you know.

00:41:38.604 --> 00:41:42.909
So it, it is sort of a content question,
and it's something to really think about.

00:41:42.990 --> 00:41:46.373
Um, that's, it's, it,
it is a bit subjective.

00:41:46.738 --> 00:41:49.743
Um, so it's something for your
content people to, to think about.

00:41:50.105 --> 00:41:54.808
Um, you know, how does it… What are you
trying to communicate through that image?

00:41:54.825 --> 00:42:01.977
And if a blind person or somebody using
a, a, a screen reader, um, you know,

00:42:02.059 --> 00:42:03.363
are they better off knowing about it?

00:42:03.383 --> 00:42:04.287
Should they know about it?

00:42:04.534 --> 00:42:08.194
Um, if there's text in the image,
don't do that, but if there is

00:42:08.205 --> 00:42:11.162
then definitely have it, alt text.

00:42:11.163 --> 00:42:13.819
So thinking about intent, I
think is, is what I would say.

00:42:15.185 --> 00:42:15.562
Abigail: Yeah.

00:42:15.568 --> 00:42:16.428
Absolutely.

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:18.492
And, and that's exactly
what they keyed in on.

00:42:18.494 --> 00:42:21.033
Hey, alt text is so subjective.

00:42:21.299 --> 00:42:23.513
Um, what are your thoughts on it?

00:42:23.941 --> 00:42:25.719
So yeah, fantastic answer there.

00:42:25.719 --> 00:42:26.820
I appreciate that.

00:42:27.283 --> 00:42:32.938
Um, now another individual said you
mentioned the volume problem in higher

00:42:32.939 --> 00:42:39.487
education, and, um, they agree that this
is a frequent problem in the area, um, on

00:42:39.488 --> 00:42:42.451
university websites, specifically in PDFs.

00:42:43.270 --> 00:42:48.280
What are your thoughts on
tools that convert PDFs to HTML

00:42:48.325 --> 00:42:50.707
as the accessible solution?

00:42:52.647 --> 00:42:52.929
Kevin: Hmm.

00:42:53.627 --> 00:42:57.143
I get contacted almost…
It, it's gone up lately too.

00:42:57.143 --> 00:42:58.449
It's gotten pretty, um-

00:43:00.542 --> 00:43:02.627
Pretty prolific.

00:43:02.663 --> 00:43:05.562
I, I, I get, I hear from vendors
all the time, uh, everybody

00:43:05.562 --> 00:43:07.292
wants to remediate our PDFs.

00:43:07.306 --> 00:43:12.924
Um, I'm sure many of you out there who are
listening now live or in the recording,

00:43:13.357 --> 00:43:18.089
um, are, are constantly being just,
they're, they're, they're reaching out.

00:43:18.201 --> 00:43:25.362
Um, and I don't make claims about
anything, uh, that a vendor does on here.

00:43:25.623 --> 00:43:30.648
But I would say I think it's
important to think about what are

00:43:30.649 --> 00:43:34.647
your priorities in terms of… So we
have, we have thousands of documents.

00:43:34.657 --> 00:43:36.767
Every university has
thousands of PDF documents.

00:43:36.772 --> 00:43:37.435
It's no secret.

00:43:37.850 --> 00:43:44.405
So I think it's really about prioritizing,
and I think trying the, trying out

00:43:44.406 --> 00:43:50.382
a tool and, and seeing, um, I think
it's important, like, 'cause it begs

00:43:50.384 --> 00:43:54.655
the question, if it can turn it into
an HTML, like maybe just it should

00:43:54.666 --> 00:43:55.898
be a webpage in the first place.

00:43:56.339 --> 00:44:00.854
Um, if you're trying to remediate
something that already, uh, exists

00:44:00.855 --> 00:44:04.287
and, and you're trying to do it
sort of in, in, uh, a scalable

00:44:04.296 --> 00:44:05.759
way, then that could be an option.

00:44:06.087 --> 00:44:09.429
I think it's, you have to really
look at the documents that you have.

00:44:09.732 --> 00:44:14.099
Um, you wanna prioritize, you
know, student and, and, uh,

00:44:14.544 --> 00:44:16.542
staff-facing, public-facing.

00:44:17.049 --> 00:44:19.721
Um, but it really does beg the
question, and I, and I've been having

00:44:19.721 --> 00:44:24.333
this conversation for years, what,
like, does it need to be a PDF?

00:44:24.344 --> 00:44:25.412
Sometimes it does.

00:44:25.593 --> 00:44:27.095
Uh, most often it doesn't.

00:44:27.611 --> 00:44:32.489
Um, so that's the bigger question
is does it need to be a PDF?

00:44:32.525 --> 00:44:33.869
But try it.

00:44:33.911 --> 00:44:39.741
I, I think it's, I think it's
worth exploring it, but, um, then

00:44:39.741 --> 00:44:41.685
it just begs the question, make
it a webpage in the first place.

00:44:43.863 --> 00:44:44.195
Abigail: Right.

00:44:44.201 --> 00:44:50.027
So really just make sure that whatever
you're creating the document or

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:53.908
the webpage in is the right format
that it should be in for our end

00:44:53.908 --> 00:44:55.045
users- Yeah … it sounds like.

00:44:55.074 --> 00:44:55.271
Mm-hmm.

00:44:55.297 --> 00:44:55.629
Kevin: Yes.

00:44:56.624 --> 00:44:56.963
Abigail: Great.

00:44:56.971 --> 00:44:57.809
Thank you, Kevin.

00:44:57.815 --> 00:44:59.172
And let's go on.

