I

00:45:28.275 --> 00:45:28.899
Kevin: think so.

00:45:28.933 --> 00:45:30.261
I think it could be an option.

00:45:30.261 --> 00:45:34.459
I think, I think that it's still
critical to have a human in the loop,

00:45:34.767 --> 00:45:39.456
um, as we've, a lot of us have been
talking about AI and how great it is,

00:45:39.456 --> 00:45:42.180
and it's gonna save us all or kill
us all, depending on what you read.

00:45:42.441 --> 00:45:45.423
I really think that it's super
important to have a human in the loop.

00:45:45.651 --> 00:45:51.634
So maybe you're not reviewing every
single alt tag, um, which maybe goes

00:45:51.635 --> 00:45:52.948
against what I just said on there.

00:45:52.948 --> 00:45:57.565
But I think if you, let's say you have
100 images and you're trying to, right,

00:45:57.565 --> 00:46:03.314
because everybody has a lot of images and
no time, maybe you, you know, look at-

00:46:03.993 --> 00:46:08.741
A certain percentage of them, and then
you as a collective, whomever you are,

00:46:08.779 --> 00:46:11.698
decide, okay, we're comfortable with this.

00:46:11.828 --> 00:46:13.122
It could, it could be an option.

00:46:13.250 --> 00:46:14.857
I'm not telling anyone what to do here.

00:46:15.176 --> 00:46:18.365
Um, I'm making recommendation,
professional advisements.

00:46:18.837 --> 00:46:24.303
Um, but possibly, um, I haven't
played enough with AI in that context

00:46:24.343 --> 00:46:25.689
to really say one way or the other

00:46:27.410 --> 00:46:29.087
Abigail: The fair, fair answer, right?

00:46:29.087 --> 00:46:31.301
We're all still figuring
things out sometimes.

00:46:31.852 --> 00:46:36.794
Now, um, this individual is a university
professor and w- we know that you,

00:46:36.794 --> 00:46:38.689
um, are working at a university.

00:46:38.721 --> 00:46:41.913
How do you see accessibility
being incorporated into

00:46:41.913 --> 00:46:43.600
the university curriculum?

00:46:43.935 --> 00:46:48.673
Um, this person says, "Hey, I think
an introductory accessibility course

00:46:48.673 --> 00:46:52.051
should be foundational when it
comes to university, maybe something

00:46:52.051 --> 00:46:54.073
within English as well as math."

00:46:54.511 --> 00:46:56.810
Um, what are your thoughts?

00:46:57.796 --> 00:47:00.828
Kevin: On having an intro
to accessibility course?

00:47:01.435 --> 00:47:01.756
Abigail: Mm-hmm.

00:47:02.203 --> 00:47:06.522
Kevin: Um, so where we are here
at Georgetown, we… I often

00:47:06.522 --> 00:47:09.544
work very closely, uh, with the
Center for New Designs in Learning

00:47:09.554 --> 00:47:11.011
and Scholarship, or CNDLS.

00:47:11.547 --> 00:47:17.350
Um, and so they work directly
with faculty, so they will put on

00:47:17.694 --> 00:47:22.250
different institutes, different,
um, sort of seminars, if you will.

00:47:22.404 --> 00:47:26.138
And that's one way that we've in- been
able to interface directly with faculty

00:47:26.140 --> 00:47:28.437
and say, "Hey, this isn't so scary.

00:47:28.805 --> 00:47:32.279
Um, we know you're already being asked
to do a lot, research, tenure track,

00:47:32.700 --> 00:47:34.654
uh, teaching three or four classes.

00:47:34.765 --> 00:47:35.281
I get that.

00:47:35.281 --> 00:47:36.124
We all get that.

00:47:36.292 --> 00:47:43.600
Um, no denying that." But I think
it's, uh, you know, like if…

00:47:44.890 --> 00:47:45.915
Sorry, I lost my train of thought.

00:47:46.513 --> 00:47:50.560
You know, something that we could
start doing, I think, is getting

00:47:50.560 --> 00:47:54.504
people to realize, like, you know, if
you start doing two or three things

00:47:54.807 --> 00:47:59.089
today, it's not like… Uh, 'cause
it's an ongoing process, and we always

00:47:59.089 --> 00:48:00.747
talk about progress over perfection.

00:48:00.923 --> 00:48:06.616
So if you have an instructional design
group, um, at, at your university

00:48:06.645 --> 00:48:10.665
or, or if it's a, I don't know, if
it's corporate, you know, there's a

00:48:10.667 --> 00:48:12.370
lot of materials already out there.

00:48:12.622 --> 00:48:16.553
You know, buying, um, access
to some different curricula.

00:48:16.564 --> 00:48:21.848
I don't wanna n- like name one or
the other, but there are resources

00:48:21.865 --> 00:48:25.008
out there, so I think it's just
getting people access to those tools.

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:27.473
Um, but yeah, in a higher ed
context, I think working with your

00:48:27.473 --> 00:48:31.638
instructional design people, um, and
maybe you start with, like, a pilot.

00:48:31.638 --> 00:48:35.064
If there's disability studies, we've
also interfaced, uh, with those folks.

00:48:35.481 --> 00:48:36.716
So there are different ways, you know.

00:48:36.716 --> 00:48:41.288
Find your partners, I would say, and,
uh, you know, kinda go from there.

00:48:42.091 --> 00:48:43.348
Abigail: Ah, great feedback.

00:48:43.359 --> 00:48:45.979
Baby steps, one thing at a time.

00:48:46.535 --> 00:48:48.794
Progress over perfection.

00:48:49.156 --> 00:48:54.026
You know, Kevin, I truly wanna thank you,
